# Planning Workflow — Markdown Planning (Phases 1–3)

**Scope:** This rule covers markdown planning only. For plan→beads conversion and polishing, see **abase-beads-workflow.mdc**.

**Core Philosophy:** "Planning tokens are a lot fewer and cheaper than implementation tokens." Spend 80%+ time on planning before implementation.

---

## Why Planning Matters

- **Measure twice, cut once** → "Check your plan N times, implement once"
- A detailed markdown plan is shorter than substantive code files
- Front-loading human input enables removing yourself from implementation
- Code writes quickly when agents have a solid plan

---

## Phase 1: Initial Plan

**Where:** GPT Pro with Extended Reasoning, or Claude Opus in webapp.

**Include:** Goals and intent; user workflows; tech stack (specific); architecture decisions; the "Why."

---

## Phase 2: Iterative Refinement

**Prompt (GPT Pro Extended Reasoning):**
```
Carefully review this entire plan and propose revisions (architecture, features, etc.) with rationale and git-diff style changes.
```

**Integrate (Claude Code):** Paste GPT Pro output; use prompt to integrate revisions in-place. Repeat 4–5 rounds until steady-state.

---

## Phase 3: Multi-Model Blending (Optional)

Get competing plans from Gemini, Grok, Opus; use GPT Pro as final arbiter to blend best ideas into one enhanced plan.

---

## When Plan Is Ready

Pass the markdown plan to **abase-beads-workflow.mdc** for conversion to beads and polishing. Do not start implementation until beads are ready.
